  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Drier air continues to advect into our region \nthis afternoon with gusty northwesterly winds this afternoon. \nWinds will decrease headed into the evening and overnight, with \nsome fog development possible since we had a soaking rain across\nmuch of the area in the last 24 hours. Some isolated showers \nhave still managed to pop up despite drier air and some may \ntrack into our area from Ontario or Quebec later this afternoon \nand evening. Don't have any surface instability present to \nsupport stronger storms. Our region will remain in northwesterly\nupper level flow through Wednesday, and additional showers with\nembedded thunderstorms are expected Wednesday afternoon with a \nbit better coverage than today due to some upper level support \nwith shortwave energy passing through progressive upper level \nflow. Continues to look like best chances for showers Wednesday \nwill be across the northern half of our forecast area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A low pressure system will pass near or north of\nthe region later Thursday into Friday. Scattered rain with \nembedded thunderstorms can be expected with this system, with \nconvective weather more uncertain and relatively limited \ncompared to recent events. Still some uncertainty with timing of\nfeatures passing through, and also lacking some model \nconsistency, leading to a low confidence forecast for that \ntimeframe. Looks like things will kind of come through piecemeal\ntoo, so don't think we'll see any organized storms on Thursday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Over the weekend, much drier air moves into the \nregion as high pressure pushes the low along the longwave \npattern. Conditions will likely be quite pleasant Saturday with \ndewpoints in the 40s and 50s with temperatures generally in the \n70s. The ridge axis is projected to shift eastward Sunday and \nsupports slightly warmer highs for the broader valleys with \ncontinued 40s and 50s dew points. Overall, these conditions are \nshaping up to be near ideal weather for outdoor activities. \nHowever, there is the potential (10- 40% chance of precip) for \nsome showers and thunderstorms late Sunday afternoon, increasing\nSunday night and early Monday morning to 30- 60% chance as the \nnext wave moves through quickly in zonal flow with a few showers\nlingering on Monday. Temperatures should warm a degree or \nMonday and Tuesday as low level flow turns southwesterly.",
